def build_articles():
    static_limit = 'These are supplied resources. No game process was executed, and native code loading, overrides and effective runtime values remain unverified.'
    sdk_limit = 'SDK comments describe defaults and editable SDK attributes. Neither proves the active binary uses that value.'
    block_limit = 'Block definitions document configuration and interfaces. Generic serialized fields can exist on unrelated block types; only relevant fields are selected here.'
    lua_limit = 'Static behavior of this shipped addon script, conditional on the addon being enabled and its callbacks and server APIs behaving as expected.'
    article('world-physics', 'Gravity, damping and friction', 'Physics',
            'The SDK documents world gravity, vehicle damping and surface friction. The game runs a Bullet world with gravity (0, -10, 0) m/s², fixed solver substeps, damping (1 - d)^dt and contact friction clamp(μA × μB, -10, 10).',
            'The SDK gives gravity as 10.0, linear and angular damping as 0.1, and surface friction as 0.8. Its linear damping comment says the value tends toward zero with atmospheric height. These are SDK defaults and configuration entries, not measurements of the running simulation. '
            'A native decode (investigation/wheels.md, shown on the Wheels parts pages) found that the game builds a Bullet btSoftRigidDynamicsWorld with a btSequentialImpulseConstraintSolver and sets world gravity to (0, -10, 0) m/s², with Y up. '
            'The game_constants binder reads gravity, vehicle_linear_damping, vehicle_angular_damping and vehicle_friction by name, with built-in defaults 10, 0.1, 0.1 and 0.8, and each vehicle passes its configured gravity to its bodies. '
            'Projectiles do not use the world gravity: their own integration applies 30 m/s² (investigation/weapons.md). '
            'Time steps are fixed (investigation/update-rate.md). Components update 60 times per simulated second; the solver accumulates elapsed time and runs whole substeps of 1/60, 1/120 or 1/180 s, from the 60, 120 or 180 Hz setting, at most 1000 per tick, keeping the remainder for the next tick. '
            'Each solver step multiplies a body\'s linear and angular velocity by (1 - d)^dt, with d clamped to 0 to 1, so the default 0.1 keeps 0.9 of the speed per simulated second at every solver rate. '
            'The vehicle body build multiplies the linear damping by (1 - 0.0000224138 × y)^5.3595 below a transformed height y of 40000 and uses 0 above it, which matches the SDK comment (inference: y is the height in metres). '
            'Contact friction combines the two bodies\' coefficients as clamp(μA × μB, -10, 10) and restitution as their product, so vehicle on vehicle at the default 0.8 gives 0.64. Bullet\'s own construction default is 0.5. '
            'Wheels add a sphere body with friction 0.001 but take their grip from a separate ray request (see Wheels, grip and suspension).',
            [config(n, 'sdk-documented-default') for n in ['gravity','vehicle_linear_damping','vehicle_angular_damping','vehicle_friction']], [sdk_limit, static_limit, 'The world, solver, timestep, damping and friction figures come from those native decodes. Still not recovered: the solver\'s normal and tangent impulse iterations, its friction-row bounds, collision-shape coverage and restitution threshold, and the rule by which mass redistribution rewrites a vehicle\'s gravity.'])
    env_names, env_present = realtime_presence(ENV)
    native_limit = 'Native findings are static readings of this executable build. No game process was executed, and no value here was checked in play.'
    hull_air = 'Read in the hull air callback 0x14048e2d0.'
    article('aerodynamics', 'Air drag and lift tuning', 'Physics',
            'Hull air drag and lift read built-in game_constants defaults, including air density 1.225. The executable never reads calculate_air_density, so the realtime 0 switches nothing off.',
            'The SDK describes linear and quadratic speed factors for pressure and suction drag. The SDK labels 15 m/s as the aerodynamic-force threshold and 50 m/s as the high-speed scaling threshold. A scale of 8 is described as additive. '
            'A native decode (investigation/config.md) found where the hull air path reads these values. The vehicle buoyancy and drag routine 0x14048ad30 reads the pressure and suction drag factors 0.05, 0.1, 0.025 and 0.05, the falloff powers 0.6 and 0.4, air_drag_factor 0.2, the three speed settings and air_density from the native constants block. '
            'The hull air callback 0x14048e2d0 reads viscous_air_resistance 0.25 and lift_force 0.25. '
            'The game sets these constants itself and overrides them only from rom/data/game_constants.xml, which this installation does not ship, so the built-in values apply: air density 1.225, and the same drag and threshold numbers as the SDK. '
            'The name calculate_air_density does not occur in the executable as ASCII or UTF-16LE text. The realtime file that sets it to 0 is parsed into a registry that no recovered gameplay code reads. So the 0 does not set a vacuum, switch off air density or disable altitude effects. '
            'The realtime file also lists matching drag and threshold values; the hull path reads the native block, not those entries. '
            'A separate native decode of the wing, control-surface and fin air routine (investigation/control-surfaces.md, shown on the Control surfaces parts page) found that it multiplies by its own constant 24 and never reads lift_force, so the SDK description of lift_force as surface lift scaling does not hold for those parts. That routine applies an altitude pressure ratio without checking calculate_air_density. The only recovered lift_force read is in the hull air callback.',
            [config(n, 'sdk-documented-default') for n in ['air_drag_factor_linear_pressure','air_drag_factor_quadratic_pressure','air_drag_factor_linear_suction','air_drag_factor_quadratic_suction','air_speed_threshold','air_high_speed_threshold','air_high_speed_factor_scale','air_density','lift_force']] + [config(n) for n in ['air_speed_threshold','air_high_speed_threshold','apply_lift','calculate_air_density']] +
            [consumer('air_density', 'The hull air callback 0x14048e2d0 uses this copy.'),
             consumer('air_falloff_power_pressure', 'Pressure-side falloff power.'),
             consumer('air_falloff_power_suction', 'Suction-side falloff power.'),
             consumer('air_drag_factor', 'Copied into the hull air context.'),
             consumer('viscous_air_resistance', hull_air),
             consumer('lift_force', hull_air + ' No other lift_force read was recovered.'),
             absent(['calculate_air_density', 'apply_lift'], 'Realtime air switches', 'The hull air path has no branch controlled by these names. The realtime registry that holds them has no recovered gameplay reader.'),
             native('Control surface force constant', 24, 'The wing, control-surface and fin routine 0x1403dfd20 multiplies its air vector by this literal and loads no lift_force value.',
                    '0x1403e1120', 'f3 0f 59 0d b4 f5 7c 00', report='investigation/control-surfaces.md', literals={'0x140bb06dc': '00 00 c0 41'})],
            [sdk_limit, native_limit, 'Still not recovered for the hull air callback: its term-by-term force equation, the surface weights and normals it receives, and an SI meaning for its tuned factors. No calibration or runtime experiment was performed.'])
    examples = json.loads((ROOT / EXAMPLES).read_text())
    for x in examples:
        d = 10 + 10 * min(max((x['mass'] - 2000) / 23000, 0), 1)
        assert math.isclose(d, x['divisor']) and math.isclose(1000 * 10 * x['native_submerged_volume'] / d, x['upward_force']) and math.isclose(x['mass'] * d / 1000, x['neutral_native_volume']), x
    article('buoyancy-water-drag', 'Buoyancy and water drag', 'Physics',
            'Buoyancy pushes up with water_density × g × V ÷ D, where D rises from 10 to 20 as body mass goes from 2000 to 25000. The unmodified game uses the built-in multiplier 10; the realtime file\'s 12 has no recovered reader.',
            'The SDK documents water density as 1000 and exposes separate pressure, suction, viscous and wave-slamming terms. A native decode (investigation/config.md) recovered the buoyancy routine 0x14048ad30 and its water surface callback 0x14048d4d0. '
            'Buoyancy: each body gets an upward force water_density × g × V ÷ D. m is the body mass, g its gravity scalar and V a submerged weight summed over an eight-child spatial tree of the body; a partly submerged leaf counts clamp((h - y + r) ÷ 2r, 0, 1) of its weight, h being the sampled water height, y the node centre and r its half-size. '
            'D = buoyancy_mass_multiplier + buoyancy_mass_modifier × clamp((m - buoyancy_mass_modifier_lower) ÷ buoyancy_mass_modifier_upper, 0, 1)^buoyancy_mass_modifier_pow. With the defaults 10, 10, 2000, 23000 and 1 this is D = 10 + 10 × clamp((m - 2000) ÷ 23000, 0, 1): D is 10 up to mass 2000 and reaches 20 at 25000, not at 23000. '
            'For V = 1 and g = 10 the upward force is 1000 at mass 100, 666.6667 at 13500 and 500 from 25000 on, in internal units. A larger buoyancy_mass_multiplier lowers the force. '
            'The force acts at the submerged centroid, so it also makes a torque, and the game adds it to the Bullet force accumulator; it does not set a velocity. '
            'Which multiplier applies: the game sets 10 itself and overrides it only from rom/data/game_constants.xml, which this installation does not ship. The SDK copy, also 10, is not on that path. The realtime file\'s 12 sits in a registry that the release parses but no recovered gameplay code reads, so this build does not use 12. '
            'Compartments: a separate loop adds a downward term -g × water_density × 0.01 × (water_compartment_force_factor × W + gas_compartment_force_factor × G) ÷ D. Both factors default to 1 and are missing from the SDK file. W and G are values returned by two fluid compartment getters; G has a stored field ÷ 60 subtracted and is floored at 0. '
            'Water drag: the surface callback subtracts 0.5 × the sampled wave velocity from the point velocity, and the vertical part of that term is scaled by 0.3 when it points down (inference: the sampled vector is the wave velocity). '
            'With s the relative speed and d the cosine between the flow and the surface normal, a face with d > 0 gets a pressure force A × d^2.5 × (15 s + 17 s²) against its normal and any other face a suction force A × (-d)^0.3 × (12 s + 5 s²) along it, A being the surface weight. Water density does not multiply these two terms. '
            'A viscous term opposes motion with viscous_resistance_factor 0.5 × K × 0.00075 × (20 ÷ (s L × 1000000 + 10) + 1.5) × A × water_density × s², L being a body length scalar. K rises from water_drag_factor 1 at mass 2000 to 5 at 25000 (multiplier 4); those two mass thresholds are built into the code and do not follow the buoyancy settings. '
            'Partly submerged faces add a slamming term scaled by slamming_force_multiplier 100 and body mass. The sum of the three terms is capped at s × m × 0.5 ÷ 60. '
            'The realtime switches apply_drag, apply_viscous_resistance, apply_slamming_force and use_accurate_buoyancy do not occur in the executable; the callback gates its terms by speed and geometry instead.',
            [config('buoyancy_mass_multiplier', 'sdk-configuration'), config('buoyancy_mass_multiplier')] +
            [config(n, 'sdk-documented-default') for n in ['water_density','wave_velocity_factor','wave_velocity_factor_reverse','buoyancy_damping','buoyancy_damping_submerged','buoyancy_mass_modifier_lower','buoyancy_mass_modifier_upper']] +
            [config(n) for n in ['apply_drag','apply_slamming_force','slamming_force_multiplier','use_accurate_buoyancy']] +
            [consumer('buoyancy_mass_multiplier', 'Loaded as the base of the divisor D. The realtime 12 has no recovered path to this field.'),
             consumer('buoyancy_mass_modifier', 'Multiplies the mass factor in D.'),
             consumer('buoyancy_mass_modifier_lower', 'Subtracted from body mass in D.'),
             consumer('buoyancy_mass_modifier_upper', 'Divides the mass difference in D directly, so saturation is at lower + upper.'),
             consumer('buoyancy_mass_modifier_pow', 'Exponent of the clamped mass factor.'),
             native('Divisor', 'V ÷ D', 'Function 0x14048ad30 divides the submerged weight V by D.', '0x14048b004', 'f3 41 0f 5e c7'),
             consumer('water_density', 'Multiplied by the body gravity scalar g and by V ÷ D.'),
             native('Upward force', 'water_density × g × V ÷ D', 'The product goes into the y component; x and z are zero. The force is applied at the weighted submerged centroid and submitted to the Bullet total-force accumulator.', '0x14048b054', 'f2 0f 59 f9'),
             native('Partly submerged node', 'clamp((h - y + r) ÷ 2r, 0, 1) × weight', 'Function 0x1409424c0 walks an eight-child tree, rejects nodes above the water and loads the node weight at +0x98 for this fraction. Reading the weights as displaced volume is an inference.', '0x1409425c2', 'f3 41 0f 10 90 98 00 00 00', status='inferred'),
             native('Worked examples', {str(int(x['mass'])): {'D': round(x['divisor'], 7), 'upwardForce': round(x['upward_force'], 4)} for x in examples},
                    f'Arithmetic on the decoded equation with V = 1, g = 10 and the defaults, checked against {EXAMPLES}. Internal units, not newtons. Saturation at 25000.', '0x14048ae37', 'f3 0f 5e c6', status='inferred'),
             consumer('water_compartment_force_factor', 'Multiplies the compartment value W. Absent from the SDK XML attributes.'),
             consumer('gas_compartment_force_factor', 'Multiplies the compartment value G. Absent from the SDK XML attributes.'),
             native('Compartment scale', 0.01, 'Binary64 literal in -g × water_density × 0.01 × (water factor × W + gas factor × G) ÷ D; the term is queued only when negative. W and G come from getters 0x1402eb5e0 and 0x1402eb660, G less a stored field ÷ 60 and floored at 0.', '0x140bafee0', '00 00 00 40 e1 7a 84 3f'),
             consumer('wave_velocity_factor', 'Scales the sampled wave vector subtracted from the point velocity.'),
             consumer('wave_velocity_factor_reverse', 'Extra factor on the vertical wave term when it points down.'),
             consumer('drag_factor_linear_pressure', 'Linear pressure coefficient.'),
             consumer('drag_factor_quadratic_pressure', 'Quadratic pressure coefficient.'),
             consumer('falloff_power_pressure', 'Exponent of d on the pressure side.'),
             consumer('drag_factor_linear_suction', 'Linear suction coefficient.'),
             consumer('drag_factor_quadratic_suction', 'Quadratic suction coefficient.'),
             consumer('falloff_power_suction', 'Exponent of -d on the suction side.'),
             native('Pressure coefficients', 'A × d^2.5 × (15 s + 17 s²)', 'The surface callback multiplies speed by the linear and quadratic pressure coefficients from its context.', '0x14048d92b', 'f3 0f 59 4b 40'),
             consumer('viscous_resistance_factor', 'Multiplies the viscous term.'),
             native('Viscous coefficient', 'C = 0.00075 × (20 ÷ (s L × 1000000 + 10) + 1.5)', 'Built-in literals of the viscous term. L is the context scalar at +0x94.', '0x14048d897', 'f3 0f 10 88 e0 00 00 00',
                    literals={'0x140baf8e8': 'a6 9b 44 3a', '0x140bb06c8': '00 00 a0 41', '0x140bb0990': '00 24 74 49', '0x140bb064c': '00 00 20 41', '0x140bb0030': '00 00 c0 3f'}),
             consumer('water_drag_factor', 'Base of the drag factor K.'),
             consumer('water_drag_weight_factor_multiplier', 'Adds this times clamp((m - 2000) ÷ 23000, 0, 1) to K, so K runs from 1 to 5.'),
             native('Drag mass thresholds', {'lower': 2000, 'upper': 23000}, 'Built-in literals for K, independent of buoyancy_mass_modifier_lower and _upper.', '0x14048b399', 'f3 44 0f 5c 15 d2 54 72 00',
                    literals={'0x140bb0874': '00 00 fa 44', '0x140bb08f8': '00 b0 b3 46'}),
             consumer('slamming_force_multiplier', 'Scales the slamming term, which also multiplies by body mass.'),
             native('Water force cap', 's × m × 0.5 ÷ 60', 'Speed × body mass × 0.5 ÷ 60. A larger combined vector is scaled down to this length.', '0x14048df4c', 'f3 0f 5e 3d 0c 28 72 00', literals={'0x140bb0760': '00 00 70 42'}),
             absent(['apply_drag', 'apply_viscous_resistance', 'apply_slamming_force', 'use_accurate_buoyancy'], 'Realtime water switches', 'The water surface callback gates its terms by speed and geometry; no read of these switches exists.'),
             realtime_loader()],
            [sdk_limit, native_limit,
             'Buoyancy is decoded in internal units of the native submerged weight V. Still not recovered: how component geometry builds the tree weights and whether one cubic metre of hull gives V = 1, the units and water or gas classification of the compartment inputs W and G, and compartment sealing and flooding. So no displacement or mass-to-volume rule for a whole vessel follows yet.',
             'The surface normal, lever arm, surface weight A, length L, the slamming scalar and the sampled wave vectors come from a surface traversal whose construction was not recovered. No SI meaning is assigned to the drag factors.'])
    article('wheels-suspension', 'Wheels, grip and suspension', 'Physics',
            'Realtime configuration separates tire damping, suspension stiffness and wheel torque scaling, but the executable contains none of these names; the wheel code uses its own constants.',
            'The shipped tuning includes suspension stiffness 0.2 and damping 1, tire stiffness 1 and damping 4, tire_max_friction 100, tire_grip_multiplier 50 and wheel drive torque multiplier 0.0004. '
            'None of these seven names occurs in the executable, in ASCII or UTF-16LE, so nothing shows that this build reads them. '
            'A native decode of the advanced wheel update (investigation/wheels.md, shown on the Wheels parts pages) found its own constants instead. '
            'Units: the working radius r = wheel_radius - 0.125 + 0.125 × wheel_size is in metres, and the tyre surface moves at 2π × r × RPS m/s, 2.199 to 6.597 m/s per RPS for the road wheels at the default wheel_size 1. '
            'Speed dependence: the slip velocity between ground and tyre is capped at 5 m/s, so the grip request grows with slip up to 5 m/s and stays flat beyond it. '
            'The request is Q = (50 × grip_factor × tyre factor ÷ r) × (2000 q + 100 u), with u the slip and q the contact drift since the last update, at most 0.1 × r and scaled by the brake. The tyre factor is 1, or 1.75 for a double wheel, times 0.25, 0.1875 or 0.125 by tyre type; tank wheels halve it outside a track. '
            'Contact: each wheel casts a ray to the ground and also carries a sphere body with Bullet friction 0.001. The contact feeds the shaft: the wheel adds slip ÷ (2π × r) × tyre factor × grip_factor × 0.5 to its RPS and submits that RPS with a weight equal to its definition mass, × 0.7 with suspension. '
            'Suspension: the ray spring pushes with 60 × tyre_pressure × (k × max(0, 1.5 × r - l) + c × (l_old - l)), l being the ray distance, k = 0.05 + 0.45 × stiffness_factor and c = 0.5 + 4.5 × damping_factor; wheels without suspension set k and c to 0. '
            'Maximum friction: the file\'s 100 is not a Coulomb limit the code reads. The wheel code\'s own 100 is the slip gain and the 100 × r slip cap, and the request has no normal-load input. '
            'The file\'s 50 and 4 equal the code\'s grip scale and tyre damping factor, but the code holds them as built-in numbers.',
            [config(n) for n in ['suspension_stiffness_factor','suspension_damping_factor','tire_stiffness_factor','tire_damping_factor','tire_max_friction','tire_grip_multiplier','wheel_drive_torque_multiplier']],
            [static_limit, 'The units, slip limit, grip, shaft and spring figures come from that native decode. Still not recovered: the impulse helpers\' scale B, so grip has no newton figure and no curve against normal load; what sets the physics mode byte that gates the springs, and the other contact cases; track grouping and Track Tension; and the legacy wheels\' speed law. No speed or slip was measured in a running game.'])
    article('diesel-engines', 'Diesel engine connections and startup', 'Engines',
            'Engine definitions describe separate air, fuel, exhaust, coolant and electrical connections.',
            'The small engine accepts throttle from 0 to 1. Its starter description says electrical power increases engine RPS for startup. Coolant has separate cold return and hot output ports. The small and large definitions set engine_max_force to 20000 and 200000 respectively. A separate native decode of the classic engine update (investigation/classic-engines.md, shown on the Diesel Engine parts page) found that this field sets the shaft weight, scales diesel and intake demand and multiplies the starter charge request. It is an internal solver weight, not a torque in N m.',
            definition('engine', ['engine_max_force']) + definition('engine_diesel', ['engine_max_force'], nodes=False),
            [block_limit, static_limit, 'The diesel use, speed step, heating, 115 °C overheat threshold and starter request equations come from that native decode. Thermal efficiency in physical units and a conversion of the shaft model to SI torque were not recovered.'])
    article('modular-engines', 'Modular engine assembly and telemetry', 'Engines',
            'Cylinder tooltips describe manifold sharing, and composite output names three data channels.',
            'The 1x1 cylinder attaches to a crankshaft and needs manifolds for air, fuel and exhaust. Directly adjacent cylinders can share a manifold according to the supplied tooltip. Cylinder composite values 1, 2 and 3 are air volume, fuel volume and temperature. The starter attaches to a drive belt, and the alternator converts drive belt energy to electric charge. '
            'A native decode (investigation/modular-engines.md, shown on the Modular engines parts pages) found how these parts assemble and what the channels hold. Parts joined by crankshaft, belt and cylinder couplings share one crank RPS with inertia I = 1 + the sum of their engine_max_force; parts joined by manifold couplings share one temperature, heat capacity and throttle pair. '
            'Each cylinder needs the crank above 2 RPS and its air:fuel ratio inside 12 - 2t to 16 + 4t, with t = manifold °C ÷ 100 clamped to 0 to 1. Its efficiency is a speed factor times 1 - 2 (s - 0.6)², so the best ratio is 13.6 + 0.4t, 13.68 at 20 °C and 14 from 100 °C; the inspected Stoichiometric value 2s - 1 then reads 0.2. '
            'An undamaged cylinder adds 80000 × burned fuel × efficiency ÷ I RPS per update, where burned fuel is RPS × 0.000004 × voxels × fuel throttle while the crank is at most 60 RPS and the exhaust has room. '
            'Composite value 1 is 6000 × the admitted oxygen, value 2 is 10 × the admitted diesel in litres and value 3 is the manifold temperature in °C. Values 1 and 2 are per cylinder visit, not per second, and value 1 ÷ value 2 is the air:fuel ratio.',
            definition('modular_engine_cylinder_straight', tooltip=True) + definition('modular_engine_air_manifold', nodes=False, tooltip=True) + definition('modular_engine_intake_manifold', nodes=False, tooltip=True) + definition('modular_engine_starter', nodes=False, tooltip=True) + definition('modular_engine_alternator', nodes=False, tooltip=True),
            [block_limit, static_limit, 'The air:fuel window, efficiency, speed impulse and channel scaling come from that native decode. Composite value 1 is not established as litres of atmospheric air: it is 6000 × admitted oxygen, ten times the gas-litre factor the fluid tooltips use. An SI conversion from fuel to torque, power or energy was not recovered.'])
    article('electric-storage-generation', 'Batteries and generators', 'Electricity',
            'Battery capacities and charge output ranges are explicit in component definitions.',
            'The small, medium and large battery definitions assign charge capacities 1600, 12800 and 256000. Battery Charge outputs run from 0 to 1. Generators take mechanical input and expose electrical output plus a generation data output. Their electric_magnitude values are internal configuration fields. The storage, draw and generation equations come from native code and are in the Electric Battery, Generator and Electric Motor part articles. The realtime file sets electric_consumption_scale to 0.01; the name does not occur in the executable, so nothing shows that this build reads it.',
            definition('battery_small', ['electric_charge_capacity']) + definition('battery_medium', ['electric_charge_capacity'], nodes=False) + definition('battery_large', ['electric_charge_capacity'], nodes=False) + definition('generator_small', ['electric_magnitude']) + definition('generator_medium', ['electric_magnitude'], nodes=False) + definition('generator_large', ['electric_magnitude'], nodes=False) + [config('electric_consumption_scale')],
            [block_limit, static_limit, 'Capacity units, voltage and conversion to joules or watts remain unknown. Consumption of components outside the traced native updates and the effect of electric_consumption_scale were not recovered.'])
    article('fluids-pressure-flow', 'Fluid tanks, pumps and damping', 'Fluids',
            'Tank and pump outputs document litres, atmospheres and litres per second.',
            'The small tank reports content in litres and pressure in atmospheres, and its tooltip says it spawns full of the selected fluid. Pump Flow Rate outputs use L/s. Small and large pump definitions set pump_pressure to 10 and 100, but do not document the units of that internal field. Realtime tuning distinguishes gas and liquid base damping, 0.99 and 0.9999, while their flow damping entries both read 0.0005. Tank capacities, the pressure law, the network solver and pump flow bounds come from native code and are in the Fluids category and the Fluid Tank, Gas Tank and pump part articles. None of the four fluid damping names occurs in the executable.',
            definition('fluid_tank_small', tooltip=True) + definition('water_pump', ['pump_pressure']) + definition('water_pump_large', ['pump_pressure'], nodes=False) + [config(n) for n in ['fluid_target_delta_max','fluid_flow_delta_max','fluid_flow_damping_gas','fluid_flow_damping_liquid','fluid_base_damping_gas','fluid_base_damping_liquid']],
            [block_limit, static_limit, 'pump_pressure is a pump bias: the port solver adds it, scaled by electrical supply and Max Power, to the pressure difference across the pump, on the solver\'s pressure scale, which the tank pressure outputs report in atmospheres (Fluids category and pump part articles). It is not a delivered head. No conversion of pump_pressure to an SI pressure was recovered. The effect of the realtime damping entries on this build was not recovered; their names do not occur in the executable.'])
    article('sensor-units', 'Speed, altitude and radar signals', 'Sensors',
            'Selected sensor descriptions state their units and radar input ranges.',
            'Linear speed uses m/s. Angular speed uses rotations per second, and its tooltip names the component y axis. Altitude is metres above sea level. The selected short range radar uses turns for yaw and elevation, with FOV input 0.01 to 0.125 and facing yaw -0.5 to 0.5. Its radar_range field is 5000. That value alone does not guarantee a detection distance for any target. '
            'A native decode of the advanced radars (definition type 56: Basic, AWACS, Dish, Phalanx and Missile; investigation/sensors-radio.md, shown on the Other components parts page) found that they also report angles in turns, dividing by 2π. '
            'Their working range is R = radar_range × 0.02 × 0.02 ÷ (FOV x × FOV y) on the native FOV fields, a new scan runs every max(1, floor(R ÷ 2000 + 0.5)) updates, and a target passes when its distance is at most R and R ÷ 10000 × signature ÷ distance exceeds 0.025. '
            'At the SDK defaults reported distances carry up to ±1% noise and angles up to ±0.001 turn. '
            'A later decode (investigation/sensors-legacy.md, shown on the Other components and legacy radar parts pages) found that the signature is the target\'s physics-body mass, built from the components\' definition mass, and that the editor\'s FOV sliders set each advanced axis from 0.01 to 0.25. '
            'It also covers the short range radar here, a legacy type-16 radar: a target passes when signature ÷ distance exceeds 0.25, inside a far plane radar_range × (0.75 + 0.25 × 0.01 ÷ saved radar_fov) ahead, about 3875 m at the saved default 0.1, and inside side planes set by the FOV input. '
            'No scan counter precedes its candidate collection, so (inference) it collects on every update that passes its power and height gates. '
            'A later static decode (investigation/force-cap.md, shown on the Altimeter and Linear Speed Sensor parts pages) found that the altimeter outputs the part\'s world Y: height above the fixed sea-level datum y = 0, not clearance over terrain or the local wave surface. It outputs 0 above a height of 40000 or at world X above 156000. '
            'The linear speed sensor outputs the physics velocity of its own point, body velocity plus angular velocity × lever arm, in m/s. It does not difference positions, smooth or subtract wind; its four modes give the full speed, the component along its axis, the horizontal speed and the vertical speed. '
            'Both are recomputed on every logic update, 60 per simulated second, with no sensor timer of their own. '
            'The advanced radar\'s line-of-sight query filters by collision bits: an object can block only when its collision group lacks bit 0x1000 and shares a bit with 0xff, and its object mask shares a bit with 0x7f. Render materials and transparency take no part in that test.',
            definition('linear_speed_sensor', tooltip=True) + definition('angular_speed_sensor', tooltip=True) + definition('altimeter') + definition('radar', ['radar_range']) + [
                native('Altimeter world height', 'world Y', 'Logic case 18 transforms the component position through the body matrix, translation included (0x14017a230), and outputs world Y with no terrain, wave or pressure read. '
                       'It outputs 0 when Y exceeds binary32 40000 or world X exceeds binary64 156000.', '0x1403c53a8', 'e8 83 4e db ff', report=FORCE_CAP,
                       literals={'0x140bb0918': '00 40 1c 47', '0x140bb0610': '00 00 00 00 00 0b 03 41'}),
                native('Linear speed point velocity', 'v + w × r', 'Logic case 19 crosses the angular velocity (body state +0xa4) with the rotated lever arm and adds the linear velocity (+0x98) through 0x140175bb0. No position difference or sampling interval is used.',
                       '0x1403c5489', 'e8 22 07 db ff', report=FORCE_CAP),
                native('Linear speed mode', '0, 1, 2 or 3', 'Component +0x834 selects the vector length (0), the component along the sensor axis (1), sqrt(vx² + vz²) (2) or vy (3); other values give 0.',
                       '0x1403c548e', '41 8b 8e 34 08 00 00', report=FORCE_CAP),
                native('Radar line-of-sight masks', '0x7f and 0xff', 'The advanced radar passes 0x7f as argument 4 and 0xff as argument 5 (0x1403bc013) of the segment query 0x1401a4300.',
                       '0x1403bc01b', '41 b9 7f 00 00 00', report=FORCE_CAP),
                native('Radar line-of-sight filter', '(group & 0x1000) = 0, group & 0xff ≠ 0, mask & 0x7f ≠ 0', 'The broadphase filter 0x1400d9bf0 rejects proxy group bit 12, then tests group & 0xff and object mask & 0x7f.',
                       '0x1400d9bf3', '0f ba e0 0c', report=FORCE_CAP)],
            [block_limit, static_limit, 'The radar formulas and the speed and altitude readings come from those native decodes. How the body-mass producer\'s adjustments and a vehicle\'s contents change the signature, '
             'which collision bits terrain, water, glass and vehicles register with and so which of them block the radar line-of-sight query, and when within a physics step the speed and altitude sensors read the body state were not recovered.'])
    article('sensor-noise', 'Radar and sonar output noise', 'Sensors',
            'The SDK noise settings bound a uniform random error on advanced radar distance and on advanced radar and sonar angles; legacy radars and sonars add none.',
            'The SDK documents sonar_noise and radar_noise as positive and negative ranges of small discrepancies in reported positions. Both defaults are 0.001. It separately describes radar_distance_noise, default 0.01, as a maximum percentage discrepancy in reported distance. '
            'A native decode of the advanced radar and sonar (investigation/sensors-radio.md, shown on the Other components parts page) found that the game reads all three names from data/game_constants.xml, with built-in fallbacks 0.001, 0.001 and 0.01, and applies them to each output value with a fresh random U from 0 to 1. '
            'Radar distance becomes distance × (1 - D + 2D U) with D = radar_distance_noise, so ±1% at the default. Each radar and sonar angle, in turns, gets -A + 2A U with A = radar_noise or sonar_noise, so ±0.001 turn (±0.36 degrees). Sonar outputs get no distance noise. U = (random integer & 32767) ÷ 32767, so the error stays inside that band.\n\n'
            'A later decode (investigation/leftovers.md) recovered the random routine: state = 214013 × state + 2531011 modulo 2^32, returning (state >> 16) & 32767. The noise variable 2U - 1 therefore takes 32768 equally spaced values from -1 to 1. '
            'The generator runs through all 2^32 states before repeating, so over one cycle each value occurs equally often. The error is uniform across the band, not Gaussian, and the draws are a fixed sequence from the seed, not independent samples. '
            'The same decode found that the legacy radars and sonars (definition type 16, logic case 50) add no SDK noise: they write the geometric distance and angles straight to their outputs and never read these settings.',
            [config(n, 'sdk-documented-default') for n in ['sonar_noise','radar_noise','radar_distance_noise']] + [config(n, 'sdk-configuration') for n in ['sonar_noise','radar_noise','radar_distance_noise']] + [
                native('Random routine multiplier', 214013, 'The shared random routine 0x1409afa20 multiplies its state by 0x343fd, then adds 2531011 (0x1409afa30) modulo 2^32.', '0x1409afa29', '69 48 28 fd 43 03 00', report=LEFTOVERS),
                native('Random routine increment', 2531011, 'Adds 0x269ec3 to the multiplied state.', '0x1409afa30', '81 c1 c3 9e 26 00', report=LEFTOVERS),
                native('Random routine output', '(state >> 16) & 32767', 'Shifts the state right by 16 (0x1409afa39) and keeps 15 bits, so each call returns 0 to 32767; the sensors divide by 32767 and use 2U - 1, 32768 values from -1 to 1.', '0x1409afa3c', '81 e1 ff 7f 00 00', report=LEFTOVERS),
                native('Legacy radar distance output', 'distance, no noise', 'Logic case 50 of 0x1403c4d20 stores the computed distance straight to the eight-target output block; neither the settings getter 0x140294520 nor the random routine is called in that case.', '0x1403c7be8', 'f3 41 0f 11 84 9e fc 04 00 00', report=LEFTOVERS)],
            [sdk_limit, static_limit, 'The noise arithmetic and the legacy outputs come from those native decodes.'])
    article('control-pid', 'PID controller state and signals', 'Control',
            'The controller definition says disabling Active resets stored values.',
            'The PID takes a measured Process Variable and desired Setpoint, then exposes Control Output. Its Active description explicitly says stored values reset when inactive. The tooltip allows setting proportional, integral and derivative terms with the select tool. The Boolean capacitor is a separate logic component whose signal charges and discharges stored state. '
            'A separate native decode of the logic update (investigation/logic.md, shown on the PID Controller parts page) found a per-update recurrence: e = Setpoint - Process Variable, S = S + e, and Control Output = Ki × S + Kp × e + Kd × (e - e_prev) in single precision. '
            'There is no time step: at 60 updates per simulated second S gains 60 errors per second, and the derivative is the change over one update. The derivative is 0 on the first active update and has no filter. Turning Active off sets S, the previous error and the output to 0. '
            'The physical PID clamps S to ± max_error ÷ max(1e-8, Ki) when max_error is above 0 and turns a NaN or infinite output into 0; it does not limit the output itself. The microprocessor PIDs skip both steps. '
            'The capacitor counts whole updates, trunc(60 × charge or discharge time), and its stored charge is a Boolean, so it says nothing about electrical batteries.',
            definition('gate_pid_controller', tooltip=True) + definition('gate_bool_capacitor', tooltip=True),
            [block_limit, static_limit, 'The PID and capacitor equations come from that native decode and describe one evaluator call each; signal delay between separate logic blocks is a Logic category question. A Boolean capacitor is not evidence of electrical battery behavior.'])
    article('resource-trading', 'Fluid gantry trading', 'Economy',
            'The shipped addon uses fixed transaction prices for diesel, jet fuel and oil.',
            'The script labels buy gantries with prices 2 per litre for diesel, 4 for jet fuel and 0.5 for oil. Sell labels use 3, 6 and 1 respectively. Each fluid branch computes transaction as tank delta times current price. The diesel path waits for a loaded vehicle and an absolute transaction of at least 1 before executing. Its execution rounds with floor(transaction + 0.5), conditionally changes currency and fills or clears the tank. Declared base values are not the prices used by these fluid branches.',
            [lua('Diesel prices', {'buy':2,'sell':3}, 'is_buy selects the price and the UI labels it Buy here; the opposite branch labels Sell here.', TRADE, 'local current_price = vehicle.is_buy and 2.0 or 3.0'),
             lua('Jet fuel prices', {'buy':4,'sell':6}, 'Fixed branch price, not the resource base field.', TRADE, 'local current_price = vehicle.is_buy and 4.0 or 6.0'),
             lua('Oil prices', {'buy':0.5,'sell':1}, 'Fixed branch price.', TRADE, 'local current_price = vehicle.is_buy and 0.5 or 1.0'),
             lua('Diesel transaction gate', 'loaded and abs(transaction) >= 1', 'Price times signed tank delta, then loaded and minimum transaction checks.', TRADE, 'local current_price = vehicle.is_buy and 2.0 or 3.0', 'self:executeTrade(vehicle_id, vehicle, transaction)'),
             lua('Diesel settlement', 'floor(transaction + 0.5)', 'Insufficient funds returns early; infinite_money skips currency deduction; tank contents then change.', TRADE, 'local money_current = server.getCurrency()', 'server.setVehicleTank(vehicle_id, "Tank", 0, NITROGEN)'),
             lua('Buy label', 'Buy here / L', 'UI confirms is_buy branch terminology.', TRADE, 'if vehicle.is_buy then\r\n                server.addMapLabel', 'getPurchaseColour())')],
            [lua_limit, 'Displayed currency-per-litre prices are addon behavior, not universal engine economy. Availability depends on spawned gantries and world settings. No live transaction was tested.'])
    store = storage_native()
    article('resource-storage', 'Base fuel inventory transfers', 'Economy',
            'The storage addon transfers fuel between named vehicle tanks and tile inventories, each port once every 60 onTick callbacks.',
            'Storage staggers ports by vehicle ID modulo 60 against a callback counter, so a port is handled on one onTick callback in 60. The counter ignores game_ticks, so the period is 60 callbacks; how long that takes depends on how often the engine calls onTick. '
            'Diesel input adds the tank\'s whole diesel entry to the tile inventory, with no per-callback limit, then requests 0 fresh water and 0 nitrogen in the inlet. Jet input does the same with the jet fuel entry. '
            'Diesel output computes the free space as capacity minus the tank\'s total value, clamps it to the tile\'s diesel stock, subtracts that delta from the stock and requests old total plus delta of the tank\'s current dominant fluid_type, then 0 nitrogen. Jet output does the same with jet fuel. '
            'Inputs run before outputs, so fuel credited by an inlet can leave through an outlet in the same callback. Example: an outlet of capacity 100 holding 40 with 25 in stock requests 65 and leaves 0 in stock. '
            'The output request keeps the tank\'s existing fluid_type, so a diesel outlet holding mostly jet fuel debits diesel stock while asking for more of the jet fuel; the input clears fresh water and nitrogen, not the fuel it credited. '
            f'A native decode ({SMALL_PARTS}) found that server.setVehicleTank does not write the tank itself. Its binding resolves the named tank and appends a 36-byte request, kind 2, holding the vehicle ID, voxel position, value and fluid ID, to a queue; the tank changes only when that queue is applied. '
            'This is addon bookkeeping rather than the fluid network solver.',
            [lua('Port scheduling', 'port.id % 60 == tick', 'Counter increments per onTick callback and wraps at 60; game_ticks is not used by this counter.', STORAGE, 'function onTick(game_ticks)', 'if port.id % 60 == tick then'),
             lua('Diesel input', 'd = d + tank_data.values[1]', 'Uses the diesel species entry, then requests 0 of fluid 0 and 0 of nitrogen in the inlet.', STORAGE, 'local tank_data, success = server.getVehicleTank(port.id, "diesel_in")', 'is_update = true'),
             lua('Jet input', 'j = j + tank_data2.values[2]', 'Uses the jet fuel species entry, then the same two zero requests.', STORAGE, 'local tank_data2, success2 = server.getVehicleTank(port.id, "jet_in")', 'is_update = true'),
             lua('Diesel output', 'delta = min(capacity - value, d)', 'Transfers only when space and inventory allow; source uses total tank value to compute space and requests value + delta of the existing fluid_type.', STORAGE, 'local tank_data, success = server.getVehicleTank(port.id, "diesel_out")', 'is_update = true'),
             lua('Jet output', 'delta = min(capacity - value, j)', 'Same rule against jet fuel stock.', STORAGE, 'local tank_data2, success2 = server.getVehicleTank(port.id, "jet_out")', 'is_update = true'),
             *store],
            [lua_limit, 'Still unverified: how the queued setVehicleTank requests are applied (clamping, replacing or adding, the order of two requests to one tank, and failures), and so whether fuel is conserved, especially with mixed fluids. '
             'The wall-clock period of 60 onTick callbacks was not measured either. A runtime test or a decode of the queue consumer is needed.'])
    article('oil-survey-economy', 'Buying oil survey data', 'Economy',
            'Oil survey data starts at 25000 and later purchases randomize the quoted cost.',
            'On world creation, the addon marks deposit entries with index at most 5 as purchased. Initial survey cost is 25000. A purchase requires infinite money or currency strictly greater than the quoted cost. After selecting more deposit data, the script chooses a random next cost from 25000 to 80000. Exact-balance currency fails this source-level greater-than check.',
            [lua('Cost bounds', {'lower':25000,'upper':80000}, 'Initial saved cost is the lower bound.', OIL, 'local g_data_cost_lower = 25000', 'cost = g_data_cost_lower'),
             lua('Initially purchased entries', 'i <= 5', 'Entry order comes from server.getOilDeposits; no geographic ordering is assumed.', OIL, 'local oil_deposits = server.getOilDeposits()', 'table.insert(g_savedata.deposits, d)'),
             lua('Purchase condition', 'inf_money or c > g_savedata.cost', 'Strictly greater comparison, not greater than or equal.', OIL, 'local inf_money = server.getGameSettings().infinite_money', 'if inf_money or c > g_savedata.cost then'),
             lua('Next cost', 'math.random(g_data_cost_lower, g_data_cost_upper)', 'Currency is deducted unless infinite money is enabled; next cost changes after purchase_data.', OIL, 'if inf_money == false then', 'g_savedata.cost = math.random(g_data_cost_lower, g_data_cost_upper)')],
            [lua_limit, 'Deposit ordering and contents are server data. No live purchase, random seed or distribution beyond the Lua call was observed.'])
    article('cargo-rewards', 'Cargo rewards, delivery and expiration', 'Economy',
            'Cargo rewards combine distance and rarity terms explicitly calculated by the addon.',
            'The shipped script computes distance reward as ceil((distance / 10) * (random integer 18..22 / 10) / 1000) * 1000. Rarity reward is ceil((1 - package probability) * 12000), and total reward is their sum. Lifetime is 60 * 60 * random integer 120..240 + distance * 4. Delivery requires both distance below 200 and membership in a destination delivery zone. Loaded packages expire after full life; unloaded packages expire after half life. The script accumulates delta_worldtime rather than assuming one tick per call.',
            [lua('Reward calculation', 'distance_reward + rarity_reward', 'Exact source expression; distance is matrix.distance between terminal transforms.', CARGO, 'local transport_distance = matrix.distance(cargo_terminal.transform', 'local rarity_reward = math.ceil((1.0 - selected_cargo_package.probability) * 12000)'),
             lua('Cargo life', '(60 * 60 * math.random(120, 240)) + (transport_distance * 4)', 'Script expression, not a measured wall-clock duration.', CARGO, 'life = (60 * 60 * math.random(120, 240)) + (transport_distance * 4)'),
             lua('Delivery checks and payout', 'distance < 200 and isInTransformArea', 'Adds saved reward to currency and marks delivered.', CARGO, 'if matrix.distance(cargo_vehicle_transform, delivery_zone_object.transform) < 200 then', 'cargo_package_object.state = STATE_DELIVERED'),
             lua('Expiration', {'loaded':'timer > life','unloaded':'timer / life > 0.5'}, 'Timer increments by delta_worldtime for STATE_DEFAULT packages.', CARGO, 'cargo_package_object.timer = cargo_package_object.timer + delta_worldtime', 'if cargo_package_object.timer / cargo_package_object.life > 0.5 then')],
            [lua_limit, 'Package probability and terminal eligibility are configured elsewhere. No claim is made that every cargo addon uses these rules or that source expressions prove wall-clock timing.'])
    wx = weather_native()
    hz = hazards()
    keys = wx['hours']
    article('environment-hazards', 'Weather temperature, lighting and hazards', 'Environment',
            'Parts sample a weather temperature that blends air and water by depth: air follows a warmth fraction from world Z, scrolling weather textures, month and time of day, minus rain cooling and 6.5 °C per 1000 m of height. '
            'The native lighting table and the tornado, whirlpool and tsunami vectors are decoded; the realtime environment file\'s hour markers and force terms are not what the game uses. '
            'Two shipped addons spawn natural disasters and native code spreads forest fires; each hazard has a decoded reach or an open item.',
            'Every part that samples its surroundings outside an enclosed compartment (heat exchangers, radiators, engines, fluid tanks, steam and nuclear parts) uses one weather routine. '
            'It blends an air temperature Ta and a water temperature Tw by f = clamp((h − y + 0.25) ÷ 24, 0, 1), with h the wave-surface height and y the sample height, so the sample is pure air from 0.25 m above the surface and pure water from 23.75 m below it.'
            '\n\n'
            'Both temperatures start from a regional sampler that returns a warmth fraction W and a rain value R, each 0 to 1. W = clamp(1 − 6 × |z ÷ 256000 + 0.5 − 0.35| + a + 0.475 × season + 0.05 × day, 0, 1). '
            'The first term is a band along world Z, highest at z = −38400 m (inference: a latitude-like climate band). a is the product of two red samples of the noise texture weather_1.txtr, read at a fast and a slow scrolling offset; R comes from its green samples as clamp(5 × (g1 − 0.15), 0, 1). '
            'World X enters only through the textures. day = 0.5 + 0.5 × sin(2π × q − π/2) is 0 at midnight and 1 at noon, with q the fraction of the game day. '
            'season = 0.5 + 0.5 × sin(0.5711987 × (month + q) − π/2) peaks at month + q = 5.5. It reads the saved month and the fraction of the day but not the day of the month, so it advances over each day and steps back at midnight unless the month changes (inference from the formula).'
            '\n\n'
            'Air: Ta = max(−270, lerp(−15 − 15k, 30 − 10k, W) − 6.5 × max(y, 0) ÷ 1000), with k = (0.8 × clamp((R − 0.4) ÷ 0.6, 0, 1) + 0.2 × w)³. '
            f'Parts pass a flag that fixes w at 0.1, so wind never changes a part\'s sample ({wx["flagged"]} of the {wx["calls"]} calls found pass it). Dry air therefore runs from −15 °C at W = 0 to 30 °C at W = 1 at sea level, and heavy rain (R = 1) moves those ends to {wx["wet"][0]} and {wx["wet"][1]} °C. '
            'Height above world zero costs 6.5 °C per 1000 m. '
            'Water: Tw = lerp(1 − 4R, 8 − 5R, W) + 3 × (D ÷ 1000) × (0.5 − W), with D = max(−y, 0) metres below world zero, not below the local wave surface. There is no depth clamp and no lower floor; depth warms water where W < 0.5 and cools it where W > 0.5, by at most 1.5 °C per 1000 m.'
            '\n\n'
            'Without rain a part reads −15 + 45 × W to within 0.001 °C. At a location where W = 0.5 at midnight, noon adds 0.05 to W and raises sea-level air from 7.5 to 9.75 °C; at 1000 m the same samples give 1 and 3.25 °C. '
            'Dry water at W = 0 is 1 °C at the surface and 2.5 °C 1000 m down; at W = 1 it is 8 and 6.5 °C. These are conditional examples: W depends on the texture samples at the position, so no fixed temperature follows from the hour alone. '
            'The samplers read the regional sampler directly, not the full weather output that adds custom weather and local hazard effects, so those additions do not enter these equations.'
            '\n\n'
            'Lighting: the update divides the time of day by 3600 × day_length and interpolates a native keyframe table linearly between the first key at or after q and the one before it. '
            f'The keys sit at 0, {", ".join(f"{h:g}" for h in keys[:-1])} and {keys[-1]:g} h. '
            'The environment file instead places sunrise markers at 6, 7.5 and 9 hours and sunset markers at 16, 17.5 and 19; those names do not occur in the executable and are not the keys. '
            'The keys are lighting keyframes, not astronomical or mission sunrise times.'
            '\n\n'
            'Table: Native lighting keys\n'
            '| q | Hour | Role (inference from the record order) |\n'
            '| --- | --- | --- |\n'
            f'| 0.24 | {keys[0]:g} | dawn start |\n'
            f'| 0.3025 | {keys[1]:g} | dawn middle |\n'
            f'| 0.365 | {keys[2]:g} | dawn end |\n'
            f'| 0.5 | {keys[3]:g} | noon |\n'
            f'| 0.645 | {keys[4]:g} | dusk start |\n'
            f'| 0.7075 | {keys[5]:g} | dusk middle |\n'
            f'| 0.77 | {keys[6]:g} | dusk end |'
            '\n\n'
            'Tornado: each tornado adds a wind vector, capped at length 100, to the weather wind. Outside its core it is I × S × (−100 × a⁸ × (1 − u)² × e + 50 × a² × tangent), with a = 1 − clamp(d ÷ R, 0, 1) for horizontal distance d from the tornado top and outer radius R, '
            'u the height fraction from base to top, e the outward horizontal direction and I and S its active factor and strength. The inward pull falls with the eighth power and the swirl with the square, and the pull vanishes at the top; there is no inverse-square law. '
            'Inside the core, between base and top, gain = 12 × u³ + 10 ÷ (100 × u + 10) sizes the inner and outer core and the core adds radial, swirl and upward terms with coefficients 200, 200 and 100. '
            'The active factor moves by 0.0005 per update, and a damage branch reads the vehicle_damage setting.'
            '\n\n'
            'Whirlpool: for horizontal distance r from the centre and z = r ÷ 100, the current inside r < 200π is 50 × M × g × (n + tangent), with h = 0.33 ÷ (z² + 0.33) − 0.033, n the normalized vector (−e.x, −h, −e.z) and g = sin(1 ÷ ((z ÷ 2)² + 0.35)) − 0.1. '
            'Inside r < 100π the surface drops by 100 × M × h metres and a narrow downward term 500 × M × (0.01 ÷ (z² + 0.01) − 0.001) is added. The whole vector fades linearly from full strength at the depressed surface to 0 at 100 m below it. '
            'The environment file\'s whirlpool forces, 400 vertical, 60 radial and 50 tangential, are not these coefficients. '
            'These vectors are wind and current in native units, not forces on a vehicle: what a hull feels depends on the code that consumes them, its shape and its relative motion.'
            '\n\n'
            'Natural disasters: two shipped addons spawn them. The natural disaster addon lowers a countdown by 1 per onTick call; a slider sets it from 1 to 60 minutes, default 15, and a new save starts at 54000 calls, 15 minutes at 60 calls per second. '
            'At 0 it resets and accepts an event with chance D = min(1, (date − 20) ÷ 60), or always with no_clip on, so with no_clip off nothing spawns before date value 20 (inference: the date value counts in-game days). '
            'An accepted event is a large wave with weight 0.5, a tornado 0.2, a whirlpool 0.1, a tsunami 0.1, a meteor shower 0.05 or a volcano 0.05, placed 5000 m from player 0 at a random angle; a volcano event erupts the nearest volcano instead. '
            'This addon tests no weather, rain or wind.'
            '\n\n'
            'The mission addon chooses a disaster when it creates a mission, if its Natural Disaster Missions switch is on (the default) and no disaster is pending. With D = min(1, date ÷ 60) it tries, in order and each only if every earlier draw failed: '
            'volcano with chance 0.1 + 0.7 × D if a volcano is within 2000 m, meteor 0.1 + 0.3 × D, whirlpool 0.1 + 0.3 × D on a tile with an empty name (open ocean, inference), tsunami 0.1 + 0.3 × D, and tornado 0.1 + 0.9 × D if the wind there exceeds 70. '
            'The disaster spawns 60 × 60 × random(30, 60) onTick calls later, 30 to 60 minutes at 60 calls per second. Tornado and meteor spawn 1500 to 2500 m from the mission; the tsunami spawns at the mission position itself once an ocean point 4000 to 8000 m away is found. '
            'Neither addon starts forest fires: the fire addon only marks fires the game reports, and native code ignites and spreads them. '
            'No other automatic scheduler for the large disasters was found. The meteor and volcano spawn handlers each have one direct caller, the network event dispatch, and the other ocean event sends either answer player requests or copy an existing event to a client. '
            'Indirect calls and save loading were not ruled out.'
            '\n\n'
            'Table: Hazard spawning, reach and damage\n'
            '| Hazard | Natural addon | Mission addon | Direct reach | Damage | Still open |\n'
            '| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n'
            '| Large wave | weight 0.5, tsunami of magnitude 0.1 | none | the tsunami band | none in the water sampler | vehicle and character damage |\n'
            '| Tornado | weight 0.2 | 0.1 + 0.9 × D, wind above 70 | wind 0 from 3000 m off the top (fresh spawn) | raycast request of 2500 × S, queued as a deferred record | final component and character damage |\n'
            '| Whirlpool | weight 0.1, magnitude 1 | 0.1 + 0.3 × D, ocean tile | current 0 from 200π ≈ 628.32 m | none in the water sampler | collision, flooding and drowning damage |\n'
            '| Tsunami | weight 0.1, magnitude 0.9 | 0.1 + 0.3 × D, magnitude 1 | 0 unless abs(q) < 3.04159283638 | none in the water sampler | collision, flooding and drowning damage |\n'
            '| Meteor shower | weight 0.05 | 0.1 + 0.3 × D | one main and ten secondaries at M = 1; blast amplitude 0 from 250 × strength | explosion of strength size, or 3 × size with the tsunami flag, capped at 10 | block damage falloff and the tsunami reach |\n'
            '| Volcano | weight 0.05, nearest volcano | 0.1 + 0.7 × D within 2000 m | query radius 10 + (eruption_radius − 10) × f; heat field without cutoff | damage message of 500 to 1000 | lava and projectile contact, final damage |\n'
            '| Lightning EMP | from the weather update | none | 0 from 300 m | electrical interference, not HP | other effects of a strike |\n'
            '| Forest fire | none | marker only | spread looks 10 m around an offset point | open | initial ignition, heat and damage, total spread |'
            '\n\n'
            'Tornado damage: with vehicle_damage on, each pass of the tornado\'s damage branch picks two points near the lower column and sends one damage raycast request carrying 2500 × S, 2500 for a fresh tornado with S = 1. '
            'The server raycasts that segment, drops misses, and appends a damage record holding the amount and min(amount ÷ 2000, 4), which is 1.25 at S = 1, to a ring buffer for later processing. '
            'The damage lands on what the segment hits; it is not 2500 HP per update and not damage to everything inside 3000 m. No other caller of the append and no reader of the buffer was found, so how the record becomes component or character damage is still open.'
            '\n\n'
            'Tsunami and large wave: both use event type 0 of the water sampler. With r the horizontal distance from the event and A its age in ocean counter steps, q = 0.01 × r − 0.2 × A ÷ 60, and the event adds nothing unless abs(q) < 3.04159283638. '
            'That is a ring about 304.16 m either side of r = A ÷ 3, moving outward at 20 m/s if the counter runs 60 steps per second (inference). '
            'Inside it the surface rises by H = 100 × M × (1 ÷ (q² + 1) − 0.1) and the current is 12 × (1 + cos φ) × (e.x, clamp(10 × q, −1, 1), e.z), with φ from a 512-entry table that solves φ − 0.8 × sin φ = target. The current fades to 0 at 100 m below the raised surface. '
            'At q = 0, H = 90 × M: a 9 m crest for the large wave, 81 m for the natural tsunami and 90 m for the mission tsunami, if M reaches the requested magnitude (inference). '
            'No radius is safe at every age: a point the ring has passed gets nothing more from this event, and a point ahead of it is still to be reached.'
            '\n\n'
            'Meteor shower: the script operator clamps the magnitude M to 0 to 1 and sends one main meteor and trunc(10 × M) secondaries; each recipient gets a copy of the same shower, not extra meteors. '
            'With d the randomized incoming direction, the main starts 30000 back along d from the target, with size max(1, 20 × M) and velocity 20 × d. '
            'Each secondary aims within ±500 × M of the target horizontally, starts 20000 + U × 15000 × M back, and has size max(1, (0.1 + 0.3 × U) × 20 × M) and velocity (19 + U) × d, each U a separate random draw from 0 to 1. '
            'At M = 1, the value both addons pass, that is one size-20 meteor and ten of size 2 to 8. '
            'The velocity is a step per scene update: the update raycasts from the position to position + velocity and then adds it, so at a nominal 60 updates per second the main moves at 1200 m/s and the secondaries at 1140 to 1200 m/s (inference). '
            'Only the main keeps the caller\'s tsunami flag, which both addons set; the secondaries pass false.'
            '\n\n'
            'A meteor that hits land or water reports an impact and is removed. The impact queues an explosion whose strength is the size, or 3 × size with the tsunami flag, and explosion creation caps strength at 10: the main reaches 10 and the secondaries 2 to 8. Strength is an explosion parameter, not HP. '
            'The blast radius grows as R = 343 × age, the age advancing 0.0166599992663 per update, and the amplitude falls as 1 − clamp(R ÷ (250 × strength), 0, 1), reaching 0 at 2500 m for strength 10. '
            'Other consumers have other bounds: the body push is min(500000 × strength ÷ R, 30000 × body mass) on bodies between max(0, R − 5.714379787) and R from the centre, and tree processing stops at 20 × strength. '
            'None of these is a radius beyond which nothing happens. Block damage falloff and the reach of the main meteor\'s ocean event are still open.'
            '\n\n'
            f'Volcano: each volcano marker in the tile files sets a crater radius and an eruption_radius; the {len(hz["radii"])} shipped markers use eruption radii from {hz["radii"][0]} to {hz["radii"][-1]} m. '
            'During an eruption the volcano update queries objects within 10 + (eruption_radius − 10) × f, f a per-volcano float. Queried objects pass collision-type, random and phase tests, the phase test being (update counter + body index) & 15 = 0, so a body is tested at most once per 16 updates (inference). '
            'With vehicle_damage on, an accepted hit sends a damage message for a component or block carrying 500 to 1000 and min(amount ÷ 2000, 4), the same pair the tornado\'s record holds. How the message becomes HP is open. '
            'An active volcano also lowers the air exchange factor of nearby parts such as the Air Scoop Intake 1x1 to max(0.01, 1 − volcanic_heat² × eruption_radius ÷ (2 × distance)), with no distance cutoff, so eruption_radius is not a safe distance. '
            'Lava and projectile paths and their contact damage are open.'
            '\n\n'
            'Forest fire: native code ignites a tree by setting a per-tree intensity byte and adding the tree to its chunk\'s burning list. '
            'Explosions can light an eligible tree with a random intensity of 100 to 199, a position-and-radius ignition lights trees for a queued scene entry of unclassified origin, and burning trees spread. '
            'Each tree update reads the rain value R from the full weather output and sets a threshold trunc(100 × R + 100). A burning tree\'s intensity rises to 255, then falls. '
            'While it exceeds the threshold, each tree update tries to spread with a rand() % 25 = 0 test, close to 1 in 25 per tree update. '
            'A spread attempt moves the source point by 5 × a wind-derived vector and lights one unlit tree under 10 m horizontally from that point, at intensity 1; a tree whose search finds none stops trying. '
            'Rain raises the threshold and so shortens the time each tree can spread (inference). The intensity is a tree state, not a temperature or HP, and 10 m is a search radius, not a fire safe distance.'
            '\n\n'
            '## How hazard wind and current push a vehicle'
            '\n\n'
            'Hazard wind and current enter as velocities, not forces. The vehicle update samples the weather wind, tornado wind included, and the hull air callback uses the point velocity v + ω × r minus that wind at each surface point; the water callback subtracts the sampled current the same way. '
            'Each callback writes a force and lever arm per surface point. A pass sums the forces and the torques r × F, and the vehicle submits both to its Bullet body, which adds them to the body\'s total force and torque. '
            'The solver turns total force into a velocity change: for a free body with unit linear factors, Δv = F × dt ÷ m, which is F ÷ (60 × m) at dt = 1/60 s. '
            'The push therefore depends on the hull\'s surfaces, shielding, air or water density, drag and lift terms and its own motion. A stationary, unshielded surface sees air moving at minus the sampled wind. '
            'No single force or acceleration applies to every vehicle at a given distance from a tornado; to isolate a tornado\'s push on one vehicle, compare the surface forces with and without its wind (inference). The wind cap of 100 limits the velocity, not the force. '
            'Each reach above bounds one direct contribution. None is a safe distance from debris, projectiles, collisions, secondary events or ordinary weather.',
            [config(n) for n in ['sunrise_start_hour','sunrise_hour','sunrise_end_hour','sunset_start_hour','sunset_hour','sunset_end_hour','tornado_base_radius_inner','tornado_base_radius_outer','tornado_wind_component_vertical','tornado_wind_component_radial','tornado_wind_component_tangential','whirlpool_vertical_force','whirlpool_radial_force','whirlpool_tangent_force']] +
            [absent([n for n in env_names if n.startswith(('sunrise_', 'sunset_', 'tornado_', 'whirlpool_'))], 'Lighting and hazard names', 'None of the sunrise, sunset, tornado or whirlpool names in the environment file occurs in the executable.'),
             realtime_loader()] + wx['temperature'] + wx['lighting'] + wx['tornado'] + wx['whirlpool'] + hz['spawning'] + hz['tornado'] + hz['ocean'] + hz['meteor'] + hz['volcano'] + hz['fire'] + hz['scheduling'] + hz['force'],
            [native_limit,
             'The texture values at named map sites were not recovered, so no site-specific temperature is given. The month numbering and calendar labels were not established, so which in-game month has the warmest season term is not known.',
             'Final vehicle and character damage was not traced: the consumer of the tornado\'s deferred damage record, how the volcano damage message becomes HP, and any damage from whirlpool and tsunami water beyond collision, flooding or drowning.',
             'Meteor block damage falloff, the largest radius of every explosion consumer and the reach of the main meteor\'s ocean event were not established.',
             'Volcano lava and projectile paths and their contact damage, the full eruption acceptance test and its height-map helper were not decoded.',
             'Forest fire scheduling and damage were not established: how initial fires start, including the lightning and explosion ignition chances, how often the tree update runs, vehicle and character heat and damage, and how far a fire spreads over several generations.',
             'Whether native code schedules natural disasters besides the two addons was not established: indirect calls, save restore paths and every writer of the volcano activation flag were not ruled out.',
             'The wind and current force chain was not traced in full: the cached current grid, surface geometry weights, shielding eligibility, other wind and current consumers, and the kilograms per editor mass unit.'])
    article('jet-engines', 'Jet startup, turbines and afterburners', 'Engines',
            'Jet component descriptions separate electrical startup, combustion and exhaust controls.',
            'The compressor requires electrical power to begin compression. The combustion chamber takes jet fuel and throttle from 0 to 1, and its tooltip says combustion begins when internal pressure is high enough without giving a threshold. Turbines convert air pressure to RPS. The medium turbine documents a torque connection for motor startup or driving props and generators. The afterburner adds thrust at increased fuel consumption. Its thrust spoiler input runs from 0 for no reduction to 1 for total reduction. The separate Turbine Engine part (turbine.xml) is not a jet part: a native decode (investigation/classic-engines.md) found it runs the classic diesel engine update, so its engine_max_force of 100000 says nothing about jet turbines. A native decode of the modular jet update (investigation/jet-engines.md, shown on the Jet engines parts pages) found that combustion needs internal RPS strictly above 0.5, not a pressure threshold. The effective throttle u is clamped to 0 to 1 and limited by the jet fuel in the chamber, which burns 0.12 × u × RPS L/s. The compressor, enabled and powered, requests 0.1 charge per update, transfers 0.2 × (100 × r0 - (p_out - p_in)) of pressure state and loses 0.0001 times that transfer in RPS after its starter input and 0.99 retention. Combustion adds 1600 × u × RPS pressure and 0.1 × u × RPS internal temperature per update. Turbines halve the incoming pressure, gain 0.0000375 × pressure RPS when undamaged and charge their Electric connection. The power connection of the medium turbine submits its RPS with shaft weight 160. Exhaust force is 32 × (incoming pressure - 1000), scaled by altitude, the thrust spoiler and an afterburner multiplier of 1 + 500 × the jet fuel stored in the exhaust, and capped at 100 × S, S being the summed adjusted mass of the bodies joined by joints (investigation/force-cap.md). RPS reaches the exhaust only through the pressure the chamber adds. '
            'A later decode (investigation/leftovers.md) found no jet-fuel debit in the afterburner: its Fuel port exchange fills the buffer or returns fuel, and its purge of other liquids skips jet fuel, so with the buffer level steady the afterburner draws 0 L/s. '
            'The tooltip\'s increased fuel consumption has no counterpart in that code; more stored fuel raises thrust, not burn.',
            definition('jet_engine_compressor', tooltip=True) + definition('jet_engine_combustion_chamber', tooltip=True) + definition('jet_engine_turbine_small', tooltip=True, short_tooltip=True) + definition('jet_engine_turbine_medium', tooltip=True) + definition('jet_engine_exhaust_afterburner', tooltip=True) + [
                native('Afterburner purge call', 'kept index 2, fraction 0.001', 'The exhaust update calls the purge helper 0x1402ebdc0, which removes other liquids into a discarded container.', '0x140351dfc', 'e8 bf 9f f9 ff', report=LEFTOVERS),
                native('Purge skips jet fuel', 'index 2 kept', 'cmp r8d,2 followed by je at 0x1402ebe35 jumps over the debit block 0x1402ebe37-0x1402ebe60 for jet fuel.', '0x1402ebe31', '41 83 f8 02', report=LEFTOVERS),
                native('Port exchange moves jet fuel both ways', 'one side - q, other side + q', 'The port exchange helper 0x1402ec710 subtracts moved jet fuel (container +0x14) from one side and adds it to the other (0x1402ecb61); it is transport, not combustion.', '0x1402ecb4a', 'f3 0f 11 41 14', report=LEFTOVERS)],
            [block_limit, static_limit, 'The ignition, fuel, compressor, combustion, turbine and exhaust equations come from that native decode. Physical units of jet pressure and temperature were not established, and SI torque, power and efficiency were not recovered. The equilibrium and startup time of an assembled engine were not traced, because how ducts and branches connect the parts was not reconstructed. The medium turbine port described as torque carries RPS with solver weight 160, not a torque in N m.'])
    article('steam-heat-cycle', 'Fireboxes, boilers and condensers', 'Engines',
            'A firebox burns at most 0.00002 × voxels coal per update and heats its coolant; boilers and condensers convert water and steam at a fixed temperature of 100, at min(0.001 × (T - 100), water) per update when boiling.',
            'The coal firebox consumes one coal to ignite and provides air, exhaust and coolant connections. The boiler tooltip recommends a firebox heat supply and controlling evaporation to maintain consistent output steam pressure. Its labelled Water In and Steam Out ports have no descriptions. The condenser converts steam back to water and should be continuously cooled; its coolant descriptions explicitly call for cold fluid. '
            'A native decode (investigation/steam.md, shown on the Steam parts pages) found that the firebox requests 0.00002 × voxels × clamp((600 - T) ÷ 400, 0, 1) coal per update, at most 0.054 and 0.21 per simulated second for the small and large firebox. It burns only while Air pressure is more than 1 above Exhaust pressure and oxygen covers the request at a 0.21 reference fraction, and each unit of coal burned adds 200 to the fire temperature. '
            'The fire loses 0.001 of its gap to the surroundings per update and exchanges heat with its coolant at k = 0.0008 × voxels, the fire weighted 400. '
            'Boilers and condensers convert in their phase chamber at a fixed temperature of 100, whatever the pressure: at or above 100, min(0.001 × (T - 100), water) per update becomes steam, 1:1 by stored amount; below 100, min(0.001 × (100 - T), 0.1 × steam) condenses. '
            'Phase and coolant chambers exchange heat at k = 0.1 × (1 - D), D = clamp(0.1 × damage, 0, 1), weighted by liquid amount, and lose 0.0001 of their gap to the surroundings per update. '
            'Boiler Water In admits fresh water one way in with bias -60, and Steam Out passes steam one way out with bias 2.5 × steam ÷ phase volume; the condenser\'s Steam In and Water Out are the reverse with no bias, and the coolant ports enforce no direction. '
            'The port solver\'s 0 to 60 target clamp is a flow rule, not a pressure limit, and the boiler, condenser and phase code contain no pressure limit or burst test.',
            definition('steam_coal_firebox', tooltip=True) + definition('steam_boiler', tooltip=True, labels=True, short_tooltip=True) + definition('steam_condenser', tooltip=True, labels=True, short_tooltip=True),
            [block_limit, static_limit, 'The burn, phase, heat-transfer and port figures come from that native decode, not from these definitions alone. Still not decoded: what counts down or clears the firebox ignition state, any global rupture or explosion path beyond a damage flag on hot fireboxes, and a joule or thermal-unit calibration of coal heat and boiling. Temperatures and amounts are internal game values.'])
    article('steam-mechanical-output', 'Steam turbines and piston timing', 'Engines',
            'The turbine turns steam flow into a target RPS that keeps 0.985 per update; the piston switches its own ports at half turn and drives its crank by 0.002 × steam pressure difference × sin(2πz).',
            'The turbine tooltip says torque comes from the force generated by steam passing through it. Its Steam In, Steam Out and RPS ports are labels without descriptions. The piston tooltip says outer-coupling pressure extends the piston and inner-coupling pressure retracts it. Alternating pressure according to piston position allows the crankpin to rotate continuously. The piston also has labelled Piston Position and RPS signals and two described power connections. '
            'A native decode (investigation/steam.md, shown on the Steam parts pages) found that the turbine passes gas one way from Steam In to Steam Out through the pump solver with flow factor 0.0025, so exhaust pressure at or above inlet pressure stops it, and it consumes no steam. '
            'Each update its speed gains 1000 × the steam moved, is multiplied by 0.985 and is submitted as target RPS with shaft weight 65 on each RPS port; unloaded it settles at 0.985 ÷ 0.015 × 1000 × the steam moved per update. '
            'The piston\'s crank phase z runs 0 to 1 turns and is the Piston Position output. Each chamber has a Steam In and a Steam Out, and the update opens one of each pair by half turn, switching at z = 0.5, so no external valve timing is needed. '
            'Each update it adds 0.002 × (p_B - p_A) × sin(2πz) to its crank group, p being chamber pressure times steam fraction, and submits the group speed with shaft weight 20 × voxels per port. '
            'The render crank radii 0.075, 0.146 and 0.216 suggest strokes of 0.15, 0.292 and 0.432 world units (inference).',
            definition('steam_turbine', tooltip=True, labels=True) + definition('steam_piston', tooltip=True, labels=True),
            [block_limit, static_limit, 'The turbine and piston figures come from that native decode, not from these definitions alone. Still not decoded: how a piston crank group integrates its accumulated drive into phase and loaded shaft speed, and any N m torque or conversion-efficiency calibration; turbine and piston outputs are shaft-solver speeds and weights, not torques. Repeated port labels are preserved; the piston\'s first and second Steam In and Steam Out are matched to its chambers by definition order (inference).'])
    nuc, nf = nuclear_native()
    article('nuclear-reactor-components', 'Nuclear fuel assemblies and control rods', 'Engines',
            'Fuel assemblies add the reactions of up to four coupled fuel assemblies, control rods scale the excess over 0.1 × fuel by 1 − 0.01 × insertion, and fuel rods heat by 0.1 × r per update and take damage above 1500.',
            'A fuel rod can be inserted into a fuel assembly to begin fission. The assembly tooltip says fuel rods consume uranium ingots from workbench inventory to fuel. A control rod decreases the reaction rate of adjacent rods. The assembly labels Release Fuel Rod and Fuel Rod Temperature, while the control rod labels Insertion Target and Insertion; their port descriptions are empty. '
            f'A native decode ({NUCLEAR}, shown on the Nuclear parts pages) names the three nuclear_component_type values from the editor selector: 0 fuel assembly, 1 fuel rod and 2 control rod. '
            'Each update an assembly with a rod computes A = b + 0.999 × (R − b), B = S + 0.998 × (A − S) and b + (1 − 0.01 × C) × (B − b), with b = 0.1 × fuel fraction, R the rod\'s reaction, S the summed reaction of coupled fuel assemblies and C the summed insertion of coupled control rods; the rod then clamps its reaction r to 0 to 50. '
            'Adjacency is the four control_rod couplings x+, x-, z+ and z-, not a radius, so an assembly sees at most four neighbours, fuel or control. '
            f"Insertion follows Insertion Target clamped to 0 to 1 by at most 0.005 per update, {nf['updates']} updates or {g(nf['stroke'], 4)} s for a full stroke; larger insertion lowers the reaction but never below the 0.1 × fuel baseline. "
            f"A rod uses 1e-8 × r of its fuel per update, so a full rod lasts {g(nf['life'], 5)} s, {g(nf['life'] / 3600, 4)} simulated hours, at r = 50. "
            'It heats by 0.1 × r per update, exchanges heat with immersed coolant at k = 0.001 × (q ÷ A) × w, the rod counting as 100 liquid amount and its own step capped at 0.5, and moves 0.001 of the way to its surroundings. '
            'A rod above r = 0.001 is a live radiation source of strength s = 0.1 × r with linear falloff over 10 × s metres, cut by 200 m across compartments, and assemblies add 0.001 × reaction ÷ A to their coolant\'s contamination, which a radiation detector inside reads. '
            'Strictly above 1500 a rod takes damage at once and then every 300 hot updates, up to 50; the update has no separate meltdown or explosion temperature.',
            definition('steam_nuclear_fuel_rod', nodes=False, tooltip=True) + definition('steam_nuclear_fuel_assembly', tooltip=True, labels=True) + definition('steam_nuclear_control_rod', tooltip=True, labels=True) + nuc,
            [block_limit, static_limit, 'The reaction, coupling, heat, radiation and damage figures come from that native decode, not from these definitions alone. Still not decoded: ' + '; '.join(nf['open']) + '. '
             'Temperatures, reaction and detector readings are internal game values.'])
    article('transmission-gears-clutches', 'Gear ratios and clutch engagement', 'Engines',
            'Gearboxes pull RPS B toward the selected ratio × RPS A, so for N:1 the A side is fast; the standalone clutch couples with strength input^6 and the modular clutch follows input^3 with a slew.',
            'The selected gearbox definitions expose power connections A and B, an electrical connection and a Gear Switch input. Their tooltips say the switch toggles between two property values. The torque_gearbox_2 tooltip states an operating torque difference up to 4000 without specifying a unit. The standard clutch takes engagement from 0 to 1 and says this controls transmitted power. The modular clutch attaches to a crankshaft to control torque made available to the connected system. '
            'A native decode (investigation/transmission.md, shown on the Transmission parts pages) found that every gearbox offers the same eight ratios: 1:-1, 1:1, 6:5, 3:2, 9:5, 2:1, 5:2 and 3:1, as multipliers q = -1, 1, 5/6, 2/3, 5/9, 1/2, 2/5 and 1/3. '
            'The gearbox stores q on RPS A and 1 ÷ q on RPS B, and the shaft solver pulls RPS B toward q × RPS A, so for an N:1 option RPS A is the fast side and RPS B targets A ÷ N. '
            'The two sides stay separate shaft groups. Each update a group adds the other side\'s speed with path weight h = 0.95 × c, where c is 1 for an intact gearbox, adding sign(q) × v × I × h to its numerator and I × h ÷ |q| to its weight; paths with h at or below 0.001 add nothing. '
            'This is a weighted speed exchange with no slip threshold, and 0.95 is not a 95% power efficiency: two groups already at the ratio stay there. '
            'Gear Switch picks Ratio Off or Ratio On, and a new ratio replaces the old one in the same update while the gearbox has any electrical availability; without power it keeps the last ratio and stays coupled. '
            'The standalone clutch sets c = e × clamp(input, 0, 1)^6 directly each update, with e = min(1, availability ÷ 0.01), so input 0.5 gives 1/64 and an isolated clutch transfers nothing below about 0.319. '
            'The modular clutch is separate code: its engagement moves toward clamp(input, 0, 1)^3 by at most 0.1 per update. Nonzero damage sets a gearbox\'s or standalone clutch\'s coupling to 0. '
            'The gearbox force_emitter_max_force values (200, 1200 and 5000; 1000 and 9999999 for the legacy definitions) are copied at binding but not used as a solver weight, so they are not torque capacities.',
            definition('torque_gearbox', tooltip=True) + definition('torque_gearbox_2', nodes=False, tooltip=True) + definition('modular_engine_gearbox_1x1', nodes=False, tooltip=True) + definition('torque_clutch', tooltip=True) + definition('modular_engine_clutch', tooltip=True, labels=True),
            [block_limit, static_limit, 'The ratio, coupling and clutch laws come from that native decode, not from these definitions alone. Not recovered: SI torque and physical power losses, a finite slip torque or friction law, where and in what unit torque_gearbox_2\'s 4000 limit is enforced, and the events that damage a gearbox or clutch, including any torque-triggered breakage.'])
    article('marine-propellers', 'Underwater propellers and pitch control', 'Physics',
            'Marine propellers push only with the hub at or below the water surface, and pitch is a normalized -1 to 1 value in a decoded RPS and flow law.',
            'The small boat propeller receives engine power and its tooltip says it operates only underwater. The controllable-pitch boat propeller also requires underwater operation and takes desired pitch from -1 to 1. Its force_emitter_max_force field is 80000 versus 20000 for the selected small fixed-pitch propeller. '
            'A native decode (investigation/propulsion.md, shown on the Propulsion parts pages) found that every propeller, rotor and ducted fan runs one force-emitter update. '
            'Each update it scales the published shaft speed by 0.9999 to w, forms the effective pitch p = clamp(saved pitch + Pitch input, -1, 1) and the load b = (2 × w × rotation_speed × π × blade_physics_length × p × 0.75 + p × U) × blade_efficiency × intact blades × 0.32, '
            'where U is minus the hub\'s velocity through still water along the thrust axis, signed by the pitch. With K = force_emitter_max_force ÷ 1000, or F × intact blades ÷ 8000 for the pitchable propellers, q = clamp(b ÷ 2K, -10, 10) is multiplied by 10 below the water surface; '
            'the propeller removes w × (1 - 1 ÷ (1 + q²)) RPS from its shaft and pushes with 1500 × K × removed RPS, signed by the pitch, in the game\'s internal force unit. '
            'Thrust is queued only when the hub is at or below the sampled water surface; the shaft load applies either way. force_emitter_max_force is therefore a solver weight, not a thrust ceiling: at 10 RPS and pitch 1 the small fixed propeller gives about 22201 internal force, and the small, standard and large pitchable propellers about 4074, 20411 and 35002 per RPS. '
            'Pitch is a normalized number, not a blade angle; zero pitch gives no load and no thrust. A separate application cap of 100 × S limits the force actually applied.',
            definition('propeller', ['force_emitter_max_force'], tooltip=True) + definition('propeller_pitch', ['force_emitter_max_force'], tooltip=True),
            [block_limit, static_limit, 'The propeller law comes from that native decode, not from these fields alone. The cap scale S is the summed adjusted body mass of the joined bodies (investigation/force-cap.md). Not recovered: the SI calibration of the internal force and of the mass unit behind S, the separate water-interaction helper 0x1402fe950, cavitation and stall outside the smooth scalar saturation, and any effect of water currents, which the scalar path does not subtract.'])
    article('air-propellers-rotors', 'Aircraft propellers and rotor controls', 'Physics',
            'Rotor and propeller inputs are normalized pitch and cyclic values in a decoded thrust, shaft-load and force-geometry law.',
            'The aircraft propeller takes collective from -1 to 1, and its tooltip links thrust to received engine torque without giving an equation. The selected large helicopter rotor has roll, pitch and collective inputs from -1 to 1. Its tooltip describes pitch and roll control through the swashplate and says force decreases underwater. The tail rotor takes yaw from -1 to 1 to control generated force. '
            'A native decode (investigation/propulsion.md, shown on the Propulsion parts pages) found one force-emitter update for all of them. The effective pitch is clamp(saved pitch + Collective, -1, 1), a normalized number, not degrees or turns; '
            'the saved pitch starts at force_emitter_default_pitch, 1 for the main rotors and the aircraft propeller and 0 for the tail rotor, so Collective -1 gives zero thrust on a main rotor and the tail rotor\'s Yaw is a signed pitch. '
            'From the shaft speed, pitch and air-relative flow the part computes a load b, removes w × (1 - 1 ÷ (1 + q²)) RPS from its shaft with q = clamp(b ÷ 2K, -10, 10), and pushes with 1500 × K × removed RPS × efficiency, where K = force_emitter_max_force ÷ 1000 or ÷ 2000 with 4 intact blades. '
            'Roll and Pitch move the force point by -4 × input along local X and Z and tilt its direction by 0.4 × input. Thrust is scaled by (1 - 2.2413793e-5 × altitude)^5.3595023, 0 above 40000. '
            'With the hub below the water surface the scalar update gives no thrust and multiplies q by 10, so the rotor brakes its shaft; that is the decreased underwater force. '
            'A physical rotor hinge gets a motor with impulse limit 1000, and coaxial rotors add an opposing force pair of 800 × b × clamp(|pitch + Collective + 0.1|, 0, 1) about the rotor axis.',
            definition('aircraft_propeller', tooltip=True) + definition('large_rotor', tooltip=True) + definition('tail_rotor', tooltip=True),
            [block_limit, static_limit, 'The rotor law comes from that native decode. Not recovered: which loaded value selects K = F ÷ 1000 or F ÷ 2000 for air parts, the visible cyclic blade phase, whether the engine adds ground effect outside the scalar law, and the SI calibration of thrust and reaction torque.'])
    article('weapons-loading-fuses', 'Weapon loading, ammunition and timed fuses', 'Weapons',
            'Guns fire at a fixed muzzle speed per weapon class, barrel length sets only the muzzle position, and loading runs on per-class breech and receiver timers; Fuse Timer detonates fragmentation rounds, and high-explosive rounds from the heavy autocannon up, at an interpolated point.',
            'The light autocannon trigger feeds, loads and fires cartridges from connected belts or drums. Its tooltip requires electricity for loading. The selected small drum has weapon_ammo_capacity 100, reports ammo count and cannot mix calibers. Feeder belts use electrical power to transfer cartridges in the arrow direction. The battle cannon trigger fires a loaded shell, and Open Breech permits loading. Its optional Fuse Timer is explicitly in seconds for high-explosive and fragmentation ammunition. '
            'A native decode (investigation/weapons.md, shown on the Weapons parts pages) found that weapon_class selects one of seven gun records. Muzzle speed is 800, 1000, 1000, 900, 800, 700 and 600 m/s for the machine gun, light, rotary and heavy autocannon, battle, artillery and Bertha cannon, added to the firing body\'s velocity; weapon_barrel_length_voxels only places the muzzle, at 0.175 + 0.25 per voxel of the gun and each barrel extension or muzzle brake. '
            'Each update a round moves by velocity ÷ 60, loses 0.5 × (138750 ÷ (138750 + height))² m/s of vertical speed, 30 m/s² at sea level, and keeps 0.975, 0.98, 0.99, 0.995, 0.998, 0.999 or 0.9995 of its velocity; in water the step is 0.2 and the retention × 0.8. Rounds are removed after 300 updates from the machine gun and the light and rotary autocannon, 600 from the heavy autocannon and 3600 from the cannons, and machine-gun and autocannon rounds are also removed below 50 m/s. '
            'Spread is two independent angles within ±0.25 × dispersion × m radians, dispersion 0.01, 0.02 for the rotary autocannon and 0.005 for artillery and Bertha, and each barrel extension lowers m by 0.2 to at least 0.5 without changing muzzle speed. '
            'Impact magnitude is B × speed ÷ 1000 for kinetic rounds, × 0.4 for armour-piercing and × 0.65 for incendiary, and B or 0.4 × B for high explosive or fragmentation, with B from 0.1 to 4; explosions search within 7.5 × that magnitude in metres. Armour-piercing rounds lose 100 × p × mass ÷ cells of speed per struck cell. '
            'Breech transitions take 2, 4, 1, 16, 75, 250 and 600 updates and the battle, artillery and Bertha receivers wait 30, 90 and 240 updates after a round arrives, the heavy autocannon 6. Inferred from that state machine, the automatic guns cycle at most at 900, 450, 1800 and about 94.7 rounds per minute. Drums hold their weapon_ammo_capacity of light rounds, half in rotary rounds and a fifth in heavy rounds. '
            'Fuse Timer applies to fragmentation rounds, and to high-explosive rounds on the heavy autocannon and the cannons: T = max(1/60, input + 0.02 × u - 0.01) s with u random from 0 to 1, and the round detonates in the update where (age + 1) ÷ 60 reaches T, interpolated within that update.',
            definition('gun_s', ['weapon_barrel_length_voxels'], tooltip=True) + definition('gun_drum_small', ['weapon_ammo_capacity'], tooltip=True) + definition('gun_belt_loader', tooltip=True) + definition('gun_l', ['weapon_barrel_length_voxels'], tooltip=True),
            [block_limit, static_limit, 'The velocity, flight, spread, impact, loading and fuse figures come from that native decode, not from these definitions alone. Still not decoded: a throughput model for arbitrary belt networks, the fastest firing schedule for cannons whose breech is opened and loaded by external control, and the final voxel damage, armour incidence and blast occlusion applied to a target. The firing rates above are state-machine inferences, not measurements.'])
    article('composite-cable-interfaces', 'Composite data over electrical cables', 'Control',
            'A composite value is 32 on/off channels and 32 binary32 numbers, numbered 1 to 32, in 132 bytes. Wires, microprocessor bridges and Electrical Cable Anchors copy it whole, and anchors also pass a video descriptor and a private copy of the audio samples.',
            'The electrical cable anchor tooltip permits one rope logic link between two anchors, carrying data and electricity. Its ports are composite, on/off, video and audio send and receive pairs. '
            'A native check (investigation/config.md) of the scalar input getter 0x1408cacf0 found that it copies a cached value and returns, so reading an input does not evaluate the upstream graph.\n\n'
            f'A native decode ({COMPOSITE}, shown on the Electrical Cable Anchor, Microprocessor and Logic pages) recovered the value and every copy of it. '
            'The value starts with a 32-bit on/off mask, channel c at bit c - 1, followed by 32 numbers, channel c at byte 4 × c, so channel 32 starts at byte 128. Lua getBool and getNumber return false and 0 outside 1 to 32. '
            'This is the in-memory value, not a network or save format.\n\n'
            'Table: Composite value\n'
            '| Part | Where | Channel c |\n'
            '| --- | --- | --- |\n'
            '| On/off mask | 32-bit word at byte 0 | bit c - 1 |\n'
            '| Numbers | 32 binary32 values from byte 4 | byte 4 × c |\n\n'
            'Wires: the dispatcher first runs every component update, then goes through the vehicles one at a time. For each vehicle it runs the transport pass, which holds the cable copy, and then flushes that vehicle\'s outputs: each output pushes its value into the cache of every input wired to it. '
            'Inference: on an ordinary wire a consumer reads a new value one update after the producer wrote it.\n\n'
            'Cables: an anchor reads its linked peer and, while both states are below 5, copies its Composite Input value, all 132 bytes, to the peer\'s Composite Output, its on/off input to the peer\'s On/Off Output, its video descriptor to the peer\'s Video Data Receive and its audio samples to the peer\'s Audio Data Receive. '
            'Video is copied by value with no allocation; audio goes into a buffer the receiving anchor owns. Electric Cable Pulleys (subtype 9) route the same copies through their own buffers. '
            'Because cable copies and output flushes interleave vehicle by vehicle, a write to an anchor on another vehicle can land before or after that vehicle\'s flush, so the number of updates a cable adds depends on vehicle order.\n\n'
            'Microcontrollers: composite bridges copy the whole value, video bridges the descriptor and audio bridges the samples. The editor limits width and length to 1 to 6, and upload rejects anything above 6.',
            definition('rope_hook_composite', tooltip=True) +
            [native('Scalar input getter', 'returns the cached value at input + 0x18', 'Load, copy to the result, return. No recursive evaluation of upstream components.', '0x1408cacf0', '8b 41 18 89 02 c3'),
             finding('Electrical Cable Anchor: type and subtype', 'type 48, logic_gate_subtype 2', 'Rope-hook component type 48, subtype 2.', cite('rom/data/definitions/rope_hook_composite.xml', 'type="48"'), kind='component-configuration'),
             finding('Electric Cable Pulley: subtype', 'logic_gate_subtype 9', 'Rope-hook subtype 9, the electric cable pulley.', cite('rom/data/definitions/winch_pulley_cable.xml', 'logic_gate_subtype="9"'), kind='component-configuration')]
            + composite_native(),
            [block_limit, 'Native findings are static readings of this executable build. No game process was executed, and no latency was measured.',
             'Still not recovered: how many updates a value needs through cable anchors, pulleys and between vehicles, which needs the setup of the two anchor link pointers (+0xa98, +0xaa0), the full pulley traversal, the vehicle order and the inactive, sleeping and reduced-rate paths traced; '
             'who owns and frees the resources behind a video descriptor; when Lua onTick and onDraw run and what budget they have; and any network or save format for composite data. The microcontroller port limit is listed on the Microprocessor part page.'])
    article('winches-hose-coils', 'Winch controls, length and hose connections', 'Fluids',
            'Winch descriptions document electrical operation, length telemetry and hose-coil ports. Rope-node winches reel at 0.012 × cable_length × Speed m/s; legacy winches and the Hose reel at 0.24 × Speed m/s at full electricity.',
            'The selected mechanical winch raises its cable on Up and lowers it on Down. Length reports the current cable length in metres. The selected large variant documents the same controls and units. Their short tooltips describe 20m and 100m mechanical winches respectively. Both expose electrical and hose-coil fluid connections. '
            'A native decode (investigation/winches.md, shown on the winch, Hose and winch end parts pages) found two reel implementations. '
            'Rope-node winches (type 48, Small, Medium, Large and Huge at 10, 40, 150 and 600 m) step the rope by 0.0002 × cable_length × Speed metres per update: 0.12, 0.48, 1.8 and 7.2 m/s at the default Speed 1, editor range 0.1 to 10. '
            'Both ends of a rope share one limit, the sum of their cable_length values, and a step is kept only while the length stays above 0.1 m and below that sum, so a step that would cross a bound is dropped rather than clamped. '
            'A Speed input from -1 to 1 replaces the buttons at a magnitude of 0.1 or more. Holding both buttons cancels. After each accepted step the winch requests 0.01 charge units (0.6 per second); the reply does not scale the step, and with no usable electricity the buttons are ignored. Static inference: the Speed input bypasses that gate. '
            'Legacy winches (type 20: Small, Medium, Large and Huge at 10, 20, 100 and 500 m) and the 16 m Hose step by 0.004 × Speed × p metres per update, p being the supplied fraction of a 0.01 charge request made for each held control: 0.24 m/s at Speed 1 and full supply for every size, editor range 0.1 to 5. '
            'Their rest length is clamped to 0.25 m to cable_length, both inclusive. Holding both controls cancels at full supply and costs two requests; under shortage Up is served first, so the rope shortens. '
            'The winch end pieces are the far attachment and fluid end of their winch, with no reel of their own; all five end-piece files are identical, and the Large and Huge Winches both use winch_b. '
            'Hose coils and rope hoses link the two fluid networks without a length term, so a longer cable adds no hose volume or flow loss (Hose and winch part articles).',
            definition('winch_a', tooltip=True, short_tooltip=True) + definition('winch_large_a', tooltip=True, short_tooltip=True),
            [block_limit, static_limit,
             'The reel rates, length limits, charge requests, simultaneous controls and hose exchange come from that native decode. Rates are changes of the rope\'s rest length; travel of a loaded hook was not measured. '
             'Still not recovered: a force-equivalent rope stiffness, a maximum tension or impulse, and a force or strain at which a rope snaps. The rope is a Bullet soft-body chain whose material coefficients are 1 without a conversion to N/m, '
             'and rope-node ropes get an uncapped corrective impulse min(K_A, K_B) × (D - L) when stretched beyond their length; neither reel routine compares a force with a break threshold.'])
    article('connectors-hardpoints', 'Magnetic connectors, transfers and hardpoints', 'Control',
            'Connector tooltips specify attachment conditions and distinguish release from launch. Small, fluid, electric, torque and hardpoint connectors attach under 0.5 m between centres, hinge and large connectors under 0.25 m with their samples lined up; magnet_force scales only the pull before attaching.',
            'Small magnetic connectors require proximity and both magnets switched on. Electric and fluid connector tooltips describe attachment by proximity and free transfer of their respective resources when connected. Torque connectors document mechanical-energy transfer. These connectors report attachment and expose composite and video send/receive ports. Hardpoint bodies require correct alignment and transfer logic and fluid. Release disconnects; Launch also activates the attached ordnance, with a Launched output on the attachment. '
            'A native decode (investigation/connectors.md, shown on the connector, hardpoint and Mag All part pages) gives the rules. '
            'A connector searches 10 m for a partner of the same connector_type, or the other half of a hardpoint pair. Small, fluid, electric and torque connectors and hardpoints attach when the centres are under 0.5 m apart, hinge and large connectors under 0.25 m. '
            'Large, hinge and hardpoint connectors also need each of their samples (four rim points, two axis endpoints or two axial points, 0.25 m from the centre) within 0.1118 m of a partner sample. '
            'Before attaching, small, hinge and large connectors pull each update with F = 60 × magnet_force × P × W × d ÷ (1 + |d|²) for offsets under 1 m, P being the electrical supply factor and W the lighter body\'s native weight field; rim and endpoint samples pull at gain 0.15. '
            'Fluid, electric, torque and hardpoint connectors carry definition flag 0x100000, which removes the pull and makes their Release input start a 120-update (2 s) detach that holding the input keeps restarting. '
            'Attached pairs are held by a Bullet joint: point-to-point for small, fluid, electric and torque connectors, hinge for hinge connectors, fixed for large connectors and hardpoints. Its break threshold is the largest double and the attach routine never reads magnet_force, so magnet_force (0.1, 0.15, 0.2) is not a holding force. '
            'While linked, each update copies the composite signal (32 on/off channels and 32 numbers), on/off and number signals and the video source straight to the partner, with no buffer or averaging. Fluid moves between the two networks through the two-port solver at factor 0.5 and bias 0; electric connectors and hardpoints join their electrical networks into one charge pool; large and torque connectors link their shaft nodes. '
            'A hardpoint attachment sends its ordnance type code to the body\'s Ordinance Type output: 1 Utility, 2 Fuel, 3 Unguided bomb, 4 Laser Guided Bomb, 5 GPS Guided Bomb, 6 Rocket, 7 Rocket Pod, 8 Laser Guided Missile, 9 GPS Guided Missile, 10 Radar Guided Missile, 11 Torpedo, 12 Cannon. '
            'Launch sets a latch on the attachment, output as Launched, and detaches it like Release. '
            'The Mag All grabs whatever touches a 0.125 m contact sphere with a point-to-point joint. Its Force output is the joint\'s absolute solver impulse in internal units, and it lets go above 200 × the electrical supply factor, then waits 30 updates.',
            definition('connector_small', tooltip=True) + definition('connector_electric', tooltip=True) + definition('connector_water', tooltip=True) + definition('connector_torque', tooltip=True) + definition('connector_hardpoint_a', tooltip=True) + definition('connector_hardpoint_b', tooltip=True),
            [block_limit, static_limit,
             'The attachment distances, pull, joint types, release timing, signal copies, fluid factor, electrical pool bridge, ordnance codes and Launch latch come from that native decode. Free flow is tooltip wording rather than a measured lossless-transfer claim. '
             'Still unresolved: a conversion of the internal pull, body weight and joint impulse to newtons and kilograms, so no holding capacity in SI units; the end-to-end latency of signals, composite data and video across a link, which needs the input sampling and receiving order traced; '
             'whether and how small, hinge and large connectors carry electricity, since they do not run the direct pool bridge of electric connectors and hardpoints; the meaning of the mechanical bridge parameter 1000000 for capacity and loss; '
             'how the Mag All\'s contact records are produced and filtered; and any ordnance activation beyond the Launched latch and the vehicle\'s own wiring, since the Launch branch calls no firing, ignition or projectile routine.'])


DISAGREEMENT = {'buoyancy_mass_multiplier': 'Neither file wins. The game uses its built-in 10: it overrides game_constants only from rom/data/game_constants.xml, which this installation does not ship, and the realtime 12 sits in a registry with no recovered gameplay reader (investigation/config.md).'}


def build():
    CACHE.clear(); CONSTANTS.clear(); ARTICLES.clear()
    extract_constants()
    build_articles()
    comparisons = []
    sdk = {c['name']:c for c in CONSTANTS if c['evidenceType'] == 'sdk-configuration'}
    for c in CONSTANTS:
        if c['evidenceType'] == 'realtime-configuration' and c['name'] in sdk and c['value'] != sdk[c['name']]['value']:
            note = DISAGREEMENT.get(c['name'], 'File disagreement only; runtime precedence unknown.')
            comparisons.append(dict(name=c['name'], sdkValue=sdk[c['name']]['value'], realtimeValue=c['value'], sdkSource=sdk[c['name']]['source'], realtimeSource=c['source'], status='observed', limitation=note))
    return dict(articles=ARTICLES, constants=CONSTANTS, summary=dict(
        schemaVersion=1, articleCount=len(ARTICLES), constantCount=len(CONSTANTS),
        findingCount=sum(len(a['findings']) for a in ARTICLES),
        sourceCount=len(CACHE), categories=sorted({a['category'] for a in ARTICLES}),
        sourceManifest=[dict(path=p, sha256=CACHE[p][1], lineCount=len(read(p).splitlines())) for p in sorted(CACHE)],
        evidenceTypes=['sdk-documented-default','sdk-configuration','realtime-configuration','component-configuration','static-addon-lua','component-interface-description','world-tile-configuration','native-static'],
        binary=dict(path=EXE, sha256=EXE_SHA256),
        evidenceIdFormat='resource:<relative path>:<first 12 SHA-256 hex>:L<start>-L<end>; local resource references, not REA session IDs',
        reproduction='python wiki/scripts/extract_mechanics.py --check',
        sdkRealtimeDifferences=comparisons,
        limitations=['No game process was executed. Native-static findings cite byte-verified addresses in the executable; all other findings cite shipped files.', 'Observed means text or configuration observed in a supplied file, never observed execution.', 'Constants retain source scope. SDK defaults, SDK attributes and realtime settings are separate records.', 'No copyrighted game files are copied wholesale; output contains selected facts, interface descriptions and short evidence expressions.', 'Component fields are selected for relevance; common serialized defaults on unrelated components are not interpreted.', 'Realtime records are an inventory, including presentation and debug tuning. The release parses the realtime_values directory, but no gameplay reader of the parsed values was found, so a realtime record is not the value the game uses unless an article cites a reader.']))


def main():
    parser = argparse.ArgumentParser(description=__doc__)
    parser.add_argument('--check', action='store_true', help='Fail if committed output differs from extraction')
    args = parser.parse_args()
    result = build()
    encoded = (json.dumps(result, indent=2, ensure_ascii=False, allow_nan=False) + '\n').encode()
    if args.check:
        if not OUTPUT.exists() or OUTPUT.read_bytes() != encoded:
            print(f'{OUTPUT}: extraction differs; regenerate', file=sys.stderr)
            return 1
    else:
        OUTPUT.parent.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)
        OUTPUT.write_bytes(encoded)
    print(f"{result['summary']['articleCount']} articles, {result['summary']['findingCount']} findings, {result['summary']['constantCount']} constants, {result['summary']['sourceCount']} sources")
    return 0


if __name__ == '__main__':
    raise SystemExit(main())
